Yes. That pushes the center of gravity outward quite a bit.

“Certainty calibration” is one important use of your wording, but it is only one instance of a more general behavior: you use wording itself as a **multi-channel control surface**. You are often choosing or deforming a phrase not just to denote the proposition, but to control what else the reader notices around it.

So I would replace the narrower idea of “epistemic precision” with something more like **semantic-pragmatic precision and multiplexing**.

A marked word or construction can be doing any combination of these jobs at once:

- sharpen the literal meaning;
- indicate how literally the word should be taken;
- flag that a distinction is provisional or homemade;
- wink at the reader;
- point sideways to another idea already in play;
- mark provenance or ancestry of the thought;
- hang a conceptual “hat” somewhere so we can find the structure again later;
- direct intellectual attention without making a full argument;
- expose the fact that ordinary vocabulary is failing;
- preserve the particular contour of what you mean rather than merely its defensible paraphrase;
- or simply establish tone.

And critically, **not every marked wording has to justify itself as a technical innovation**.

That matters for things like *coinungue*. In the sentence you just wrote, its function is not simply “Nathan coins a new technical term.” The malformed/constructed word itself says something like: *notice the act of coining here; the lexical shape is part of what I am demonstrating; pay attention to this use.* It becomes a tiny metatextual pointer.

Likewise “pain attenexion,” “aforedemonstrated,” “interpointing of connexion”—I should not flatten those all into one category called coinage. Some may be momentary lexical play, some structural illustration, some compression, some sound/shape-driven attention cues, some deliberately conspicuous wording, and some simply the nearest verbal object to the thing you are trying to indicate.

That suggests another important rule for the model:

> **Marked language is not automatically terminology.**

A coinage may be:
- **referential** — genuinely names a recurring concept;
- **precision-seeking** — ordinary vocabulary is insufficient;
- **indexical** — points the reader toward a relationship or earlier structure;
- **metatextual** — comments on the act of saying or naming;
- **tonal** — changes the intellectual/social temperature;
- **mnemonic / hat-hanging** — gives an idea a memorable peg;
- **illustrative** — performs the phenomenon being discussed;
- or some mixture.

That is a much better fit than “Nathan likes coinages.”

And I think “maximal interpointing of connexion” captures another deep trait we had only partly identified. You often seem to prefer a phrase that connects to **several already-active nodes** over one that merely states the local point optimally in isolation. There is a kind of lexical cross-linking going on. A word can point backward, sideways, and downward at once.

That is probably why aggressive copyediting could damage your voice even while making the prose superficially “better.” A conventional editor might replace a conspicuous phrase with the cleanest ordinary synonym and inadvertently remove three of its four functions.

For scientific writing, then, the transfer rule should not be “normalize everything except certainty markers.” It should be:

> **Regularize only what is accidental. Preserve markedness when the marked form carries scientific, structural, rhetorical, mnemonic, tonal, or metatextual information.**

The discipline comes from asking **what the markedness is doing**.

A paper can absolutely contain an unusual phrase, a small wink, a deliberately memorable label, or an almost mischievously exact wording—provided the scientific statement remains legible without requiring the reader to decode the secondary channel.

For example:

> “We use *closure* here in the geometric sense; the argument should not be granted the stronger topological meaning merely because the word is temptingly available.”

That is doing science, terminological policing, reader-winking, and commentary on scientific language at once.

Or:

> “The resemblance is useful enough to deserve a name, though not yet an ontology.”

That can serve as a conceptual hat-hanger while simultaneously warning what the name does **not** establish.

So I think the deeper fingerprint is becoming:

**You do not merely choose words to state thoughts. You use wording to place handles, arrows, caveats, echoes, jokes, provenance markers, and pressure points onto the shared conceptual object.**

And sometimes you are willing to make the sentence a little stranger if strangeness helps the reader notice where one of those handles is. That is broader—and much more characteristic—than certainty management alone.

